inhibit-charge (0.5.2-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix: complete the 0.5.0 drift-timer fix. 0.5.0 added rc-branching
    on read's exit status (0=uevent, >128=timeout, 1=EOF) but left the
    `read -r -t "$DRIFT_INTERVAL"` as a bare command under
    `set -euo pipefail`. A timed-out read returns >128, which errexit
    treats as a failure and aborts the inner while-subshell BEFORE the
    `rc=$?` capture runs, so the rc-branch was never reached on a
    timeout. The left side of the pipe (udevadm monitor) kept running,
    so no EOF/restart was logged: the daemon silently stopped calling
    apply() after its first drift timeout and froze charge_behaviour at
    whatever the last apply() set. In practice a battery parked with
    inhibit-charge then drained well below the recharge band without
    the daemon ever flipping back to auto, because uevents alone do not
    guarantee an apply() once the loop is dead. Guard the read with
    `rc=0; read ... || rc=$?` so errexit cannot fire and the rc-branch
    is always evaluated. Confirmed against an independent review of the
    set -e / read-timeout interaction.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Tue, 09 Jun 2026 19:12:27 +0000

inhibit-charge (0.5.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

