inhibit-charge (0.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix: the systemd unit blocked udevadm monitor by sandboxing its
    network-family access. RestrictAddressFamilies was set to AF_UNIX
    only and PrivateNetwork=true. udevadm monitor needs AF_NETLINK and
    runs in the host network namespace to receive kernel uevents. Both
    are now relaxed: AF_NETLINK is added to the allowlist and
    PrivateNetwork is disabled. Without this fix the monitor exited at
    startup, the daemon's read pipe went to EOF, and the main loop
    spun, burning ~8% CPU forking shell utilities as fast as bash
    could.
  * Fix: the daemon main loop did not distinguish read-EOF from
    read-timeout. Now branches on read's exit status: 0 means a uevent
    arrived, >128 means the drift timer fired, 1 means the upstream
    monitor died and the loop should exit so the outer loop can
    restart udevadm monitor with a 5s backoff. Defense in depth: even
    if the unit ever loses access to netlink again, the daemon will
    not tight-spin.
  * Add: shared compatibility check library at
    /usr/lib/inhibit-charge/compat-check.sh. Used by the daemon (final
    runtime gate, unchanged behaviour), by debian/postinst (warn
    loudly and skip enable/start on incompatible hardware, but do not
    fail the package install), and by scripts/get.sh (refuse to add
    the apt source or install the package on an incompatible host so
    we never mutate system state we cannot back out of).

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Thu, 07 May 2026 06:00:00 +0000

inhibit-charge (0.4.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix: the greeting now actually shows on every new terminal tab.
    /etc/profile.d/ snippets are sourced only by login shells, but most
    desktop terminal tabs are interactive non-login shells (sourced via
    /etc/bash.bashrc, which on Ubuntu does NOT source profile.d). 'motd
    on' now drops a small block into the invoking user's ~/.bashrc that
    sources /etc/profile.d/50-inhibit-charge.sh. The block is delimited
    by clear marker comments so 'motd off' removes it cleanly.
  * Fix: the motd-enabled flag file is now created with
    root:inhibit-charge ownership and 0664 mode, so group members can
    flip it on/off without sudo. Previous versions created it as
    root:root 0644 when 'sudo inhibit-charge motd' was used, leaving
    the user unable to toggle it again without sudo.
  * Fix: postinst migration now also corrects the flag file's
    ownership for hosts upgrading from 0.4.0.
  * 'motd status' now warns when the greeting is enabled but the
    bashrc block is missing (e.g. the user removed it manually), and
    explains how to re-add it.
  * get.sh: pin the apt source line to 'arch=amd64,arm64' so apt does
    not emit "doesn't support architecture 'i386'" notices on hosts
    with i386 enabled in dpkg.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Tue, 05 May 2026 15:50:00 +0000

inhibit-charge (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Switch the optional greeting from PAM-MOTD to /etc/profile.d/. The
    new mechanism is sourced by every interactive shell on Debian and
    Ubuntu, so the greeting shows up on every new terminal tab/window
    without depending on PAM, the update-motd package, SSH login, or
    /run/motd.dynamic. This matches the laptop-user mental model
    ("when I open a terminal I see it") and matches what the original
    power-mode tool did via ~/.bashrc.
  * Reposition the feature in the docs as user UI candy, not a system
    MOTD. Drop all language about "login session", "fresh login",
    "PAM", and "/etc/update-motd.d/". Make clear that this is for
    laptop users opening terminals, not headless-server logins.
  * Drop the Recommends: update-motd dependency. The new mechanism
    needs no external package.
  * postinst migrates from <0.4.0: removes the old
    /etc/update-motd.d/50-inhibit-charge file on upgrade.
  * The toggle is now a flag file at /var/lib/inhibit-charge/motd-enabled.
    Group members of 'inhibit-charge' can flip it without sudo.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Tue, 05 May 2026 15:30:00 +0000

inhibit-charge (0.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Recommends: update-motd. On Debian/Ubuntu, PAM reads MOTD from
    /run/motd.dynamic, which is generated by the update-motd package.
    Without that package the MOTD entry is on disk but PAM has nothing
    to display. Recommending it means apt pulls it in by default.
  * 'inhibit-charge motd' now detects the missing renderer at toggle
    time and prints a clear pointer to 'sudo apt install update-motd',
    instead of silently appearing to "do nothing".
  * Remove the misleading 'bash -l' hint from CLI output, README, and
    project page. 'bash -l' is a regular interactive login shell that
    does NOT trigger pam_motd, so it never prints MOTD. The correct
    triggers are SSH login, console login, and display-manager login.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Tue, 05 May 2026 15:10:00 +0000

inhibit-charge (0.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * New: optional MOTD entry. The package now ships
    /etc/update-motd.d/50-inhibit-charge (disabled by default,
    non-executable). Enable it with 'sudo inhibit-charge motd on' to
    show the current battery state at the top of any new login session
    (SSH, console, 'bash -l'). Disable with 'sudo inhibit-charge motd
    off'. With no argument, the command toggles. 'inhibit-charge motd
    status' just reports.
  * The toggle flips the file's executable bit (since PAM run-parts
    only runs executable files). dpkg owns the file across install /
    remove / purge, so toggling never leaks files into update-motd.d.
  * New internal subcommand 'inhibit-charge motd-render' invoked by
    the MOTD script; prints a one-line summary distinct from the
    multi-line 'inhibit-charge status' block.
  * install.sh preserves an existing executable bit on the MOTD entry
    when reinstalling, so re-running install.sh on a host where the
    user has enabled MOTD does not silently disable it.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Tue, 05 May 2026 14:50:00 +0000

inhibit-charge (0.2.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * postinst now enables and starts inhibit-charged.service on install,
    and try-restarts it on upgrade. The script-built .deb does not run
    dh_installsystemd, which would normally do this for us.
  * postrm now stops and disables the service on remove (previously
    only purge cleaned up state, but the service was left running and
    enabled after a plain remove).
  * Ship install.sh in the repo for users who want to install from
    source without adding the apt source. Subcommands: install (default,
    idempotent), uninstall, purge, verify.
  * Improve "you need a fresh shell session" messaging across the CLI's
    permission-error path, install.sh, README, and project Pages site.
    Make clear that a new terminal tab in the same login session is not
    enough (Linux freezes group lists at login). install.sh now defers
    this notice to the very end of its run so it does not scroll off
    screen behind the verify block.

